    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah makes sense. i'm thinking about pulling the solenoids first since it's less work. but i'm still a bit stumped on why the pressure is spiking like that if the fluid is actually clean. i mean, usually when something's failing this weirdly it's like there's something actively messing with the flow. could it be a mechanical timing issue in the valve body or something just... completely unpredictable?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If you've ruled out fluid contamination, yet still face pressure anomalies, this could indeed indicate a mechanical timing issue within the valve body. The valve body distributes hydraulic pressure to the appropriate clutches and bands; if the valves are sticking or have worn seats, they may not fully close at the right times, causing spiked pressures during shifts. It’s also possible that an internal seizure or damaged component within the valve body could be intermittently interrupting the flow. This type of issue can be unpredictable because its intermittent nature makes it challenging to identify consistently. A precise diagnosis by a professional mechanic, who can inspect the timing, mechanical condition, and integrity of the valve body, is recommended.",
      "source": "target",
